Z||000000||kornholio||08-14-99||08:01 PM||||Does anyone else experience severe hydroplaning when driving in heavy rain?  Whenever I hit a puddle (no matter how small the puddle may be) the car just steers to one side.  It forces me to drive way below the speed limit on the highway when it is raining hard.  Any ideas on how I could fix this problem?  Is it my tires?  I don't know what brand they are, but I know that they have an S-tread and they are practically new.  Since all of the weight of the car is in the back, would adding weight in the spare tire compartment give me more stability in bad weather?  Thanks.
Z||000001||Dave85||08-14-99||08:59 PM||||I don't have much of a problem. My tires are pretty fair and I usually slow down a bit just to be on the safe side. I'd sure hate to wreck my car<IMG SRC="/ubb/frown.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15>
Z||000002||Pretender||08-15-99||12:48 AM||||with my new set of tires i would have to power break it to break the tires lose in the rain(88auto)..my old tires in the rear went bald in the middle becuase they were to inflated for the rim size, what i'm getting at is when they got bald i almost wrecked into a bunch of parked cars on my street, the road was wet and the older smother typeof pavement, as soon as my car kicked into second (not pushing it hard either) the rear end broke loose and i had a fish tail i couldn't believe! Some how i was able to pull out of it and i will never let the tires get that bad again!...in my opinion i would opt for a new set. it's better to spend a few houndred now instead of loosing your car or maybe even your life later on....L8r.
Z||000003||lowCG||08-15-99||02:07 AM||||Go to your nearest Firestone(Bridgestone)dealer the next day it doesn't rain;they(RE930,RE71,and a couple of Firestone names I don't recall) work well in the wet.<br>The weight distribution is 47/53,F/R,so the crappy front springs play a part too,but to keep it simple,I'd start with the tires.
Z||000004||batboy||08-15-99||09:25 AM||||I agree, fresh tires, a good tread design, and slowing down are the 3 best best ways to beat hydroplaning. I noticed that off-brand cheapie tires don't do as well on wet roads, must be the tread design or lack of it. I've had pretty good luck with Dunlap, Goodyear, Firestone, and BF Goodrich. Friends of mine also swear by Cooper and Hooser.
Z||000005||theogre||08-15-99||09:56 AM||||I have "response" T rated tires made by cooper for sears/NTB. They haven't given me any problem. I have to hit some really big water hard to have them come loose.
